\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Thanks for sharing this \'97 and it's worth naming upfront that these labels (narcissism, Machiavellianism, psychopathy, sadism) come from subclinical trait questionnaires used in organizational psychology, not clinical diagnoses. They measure tendencies on a spectrum everyone has some degree of, not a categorical "disorder." I'll interpret them that way throughout.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 1. Balanced interpretation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Your profile reads as: 
\f1\b moderate self-focus, higher-than-average strategic/instrumental thinking, higher-than-average boldness/low reactivity under pressure, low interpersonal cruelty, solid empathy, and strong humility and ethical leadership scores.
\f0\b0 \
A few patterns worth sitting with:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The core tension
\f0\b0 : Your Machiavellianism (64) and Psychopathy (68) scores are elevated relative to your Narcissism (46) and Sadism (43). This suggests your risk profile isn't about ego or cruelty \'97 it's more about 
\f1\b cool, strategic detachment
\f0\b0 : comfort with calculated trade-offs, low anxiety in high-stakes situations, and a tendency to view relationships or rules instrumentally when under pressure. This can be a real leadership asset (steady decision-making, unsentimental prioritization) but can tip into treating people as means to an end if unchecked.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The counterweight
\f0\b0 : Your Empathy (61), Humility (75), and Ethical Leadership (68) are genuinely strong and sit above the "risk" traits. This matters \'97 high humility in particular is one of the best-documented protective factors against the downsides of Machiavellian/psychopathic tendencies in leadership research, because it keeps you open to feedback and correction rather than doubling down.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Workplace triggers
\f0\b0 : The traits most likely to surface under stress, time pressure, competition, or when you feel you're being outmaneuvered \'97 situations where "strategic detachment" feels efficient and empathy feels like a cost.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Context-sensitivity
\f0\b0 : This profile might look completely different (and be an asset) in a crisis-management or turnaround role, versus a role requiring sustained relational trust-building or psychological safety on a team.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 2. Top 3 behavioural risks to monitor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 1. Instrumental framing of people or situations under pressure
\f0\b0  Observable signs: catching yourself thinking about a colleague primarily in terms of "what they can do for this outcome"; making a decision that technically serves the goal but that you'd be uncomfortable explaining transparently to the person affected.\

\f1\b 2. Under-communicating the "why" behind decisions
\f0\b0  Observable signs: peers or reports saying they don't understand your reasoning; a tendency to announce conclusions rather than show your thinking, especially when you've already privately gamed out the trade-offs.\

\f1\b 3. Emotional under-signaling in tense moments
\f0\b0  Observable signs: staying calm to the point that others read you as detached or unmoved during conflict, layoffs, or someone else's setback \'97 low visible reactivity can read as indifference even when you do care internally.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 3. Top 3 strengths / protective capacities\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 1. High humility (75)
\f0\b0  \'97 Your strongest score. This predicts openness to feedback, willingness to admit error, and resistance to defensive escalation. It's your best natural regulator against the instrumental-thinking risk above: humility makes it more likely you'll notice and correct course.\

\f1\b 2. Solid empathy (61)
\f0\b0  \'97 Above the midpoint and above your "risk" cluster. This gives you a real internal signal for when a strategic move might cause disproportionate harm \'97 the work is making sure that signal gets voiced, not just felt.\

\f1\b 3. Ethical leadership (68)
\f0\b0  \'97 This suggests you already hold explicit standards for fairness and integrity, which can act as a check on Machiavellian instincts by giving you a concrete reference point ("would I defend this decision publicly?") rather than relying purely on gut feel.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 4. 2\'964 week improvement plan\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Week 1 \'97 Notice the instrumental lens
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment: Once a day, before making a decision that affects someone else, ask "Am I treating this person as an end or a means right now?" Note the answer in a private log \'97 no action needed yet, just noticing.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection question: When today did I feel the pull to skip explaining my reasoning because it was "more efficient"?\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Observable behaviour to track: frequency of decisions made without a stated rationale.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Week 2 \'97 Narrate your reasoning
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment: In every decision you make this week that affects others, add one sentence of visible reasoning ("I'm choosing X because Y, and I considered Z but ruled it out because...").\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection question: Did narrating my reasoning change the decision itself, or just how it landed?\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Observable behaviour: at least one person independently repeats your reasoning back accurately, indicating it landed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Week 3 \'97 Surface the empathy signal
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment: In one difficult conversation this week (conflict, feedback, setback), explicitly name the emotional stakes out loud ("I know this is a hard outcome for you") before moving to logistics.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection question: What did I almost skip past because it felt inefficient to acknowledge?\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Observable behaviour: the other person's body language or tone shifts (visibly relaxes, opens up) after you name the emotional stakes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Week 4 \'97 Close the loop with feedback
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment: Ask one trusted colleague or report the direct question, "Is there anything I do under pressure that comes across as more calculating than I intend?" Just listen \'97 don't defend.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection question: What's my instinct when I hear something uncomfortable \'97 defend, deflect, or sit with it?\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Observable behaviour: you can paraphrase their feedback back to them accurately without adding a "but."\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 5. Short scripts\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Seeking feedback
\f0\b0  "I'm working on being more transparent in how I make decisions under pressure. Is there a moment recently where my reasoning wasn't clear, or where I came across more transactional than I meant to?"\

\f1\b Pausing before a reactive decision
\f0\b0  "I want to make sure I've thought this through \'97 can I get back to you in [an hour / by tomorrow] with my answer?"\

\f1\b Giving credit
\f0\b0  "This worked because [specific person] did [specific thing] \'97 I want to make sure that's visible, not just the outcome."\

\f1\b Repairing harm
\f0\b0  "I've realized that my decision/comment affected you more than I accounted for. That wasn't my intent, and I want to understand what it actually cost you, not just move past it."\

\f1\b Responding ethically to conflict
\f0\b0  "I see this differently than you do, and I want to understand your reasoning before I explain mine \'97 can you walk me through how you got there?"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 6. Questions to ask before applying any of this in your specific context\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Does my organization's culture reward the exact instrumental behaviors I'm trying to dial back \'97 and if so, will this plan create friction I need to prepare for?\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Who on my team has enough psychological safety with me to give me honest feedback, and who doesn't (and why)?\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Are there specific relationships where trust is already damaged, where I need repair before I need new habits?\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Is there a power differential (e.g., with direct reports) that makes some of these scripts land differently than intended?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 7. Final reminder\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Please hold these results loosely. Self-report questionnaires \'97 especially ones using clinical-sounding labels like "psychopathy" \'97 are blunt instruments, sensitive to mood, self-perception, and how questions are framed on the day you took it. They're a prompt for reflection, not a diagnosis or a fixed identity. If you share any of this with colleagues, coaches, or your organization, be thoughtful about consent and privacy \'97 both yours and anyone whose behavior you reference while working through it. If patterns here feel distressing or persistent beyond the workplace, a conversation with a professional (coach or psychologist) can offer more than any questionnaire score.\
}